JavaScript's sort function is case-insensitive by default, which means that "a" and "A" are treated as the same value. To perform a case-sensitive sort, you need to pass a custom comparison function to the sort method.

Here's an example:

const arr = ["apple", "Banana", "orange", "Cherry", "banana"];
arr.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));
console.log(arr); //
